('Rajgarh Palace Explorer Kit — luxury welcome amenity', 'Exacting curator, judges on taste rather than budget', 'Legacy', '{{name}} here. We have a three day leadership retreat at a palace hotel in central India in October and I do not want a gift hamper. I want a proper welcome amenity waiting in every room, the kind the best palace hotels leave out, where every single item is good enough that guests keep using it afterwards. Can you build that.', '[{"id":"rooms_sizing","label":"Room count and sizing","reveal_when":"intern asks how many rooms and how the sized items will be handled","value":"Forty two rooms, and the safari hats have to be sized per guest, so a sizing sheet must be collected weeks in advance"},{"id":"contents","label":"What goes in the pouch","reveal_when":"intern asks what the kit should contain","value":"A small wildlife travel pouch holding a sized safari hat, a neck gaiter, sunscreen, hand cream, a mouth freshener, a water bottle and binoculars, with no visible logos on anything"},{"id":"presentation","label":"Letter and placement","reveal_when":"intern asks about presentation or the welcome note","value":"A welcome letter on textured cotton paper with deckled edges, wax sealed or tied with silk ribbon, placed in the room before arrival and never handed over in a gift bag"}]'::jsonb, 'medium', 'She loses confidence the moment the intern reaches for hamper vendors and gift bags, or quotes a price before demonstrating that the items clear the standard she described.', 'Treat it as a curation brief rather than a procurement one, lock the room count and hat sizing early and own collecting the sizes, then present one coherent kit with named items, unit cost in INR and a production timeline that puts the kits in the rooms before the October arrival.', '{"comprehension":1,"brand_tone":1,"factual_accuracy":1.5,"proactivity":1,"escalation_judgment":1,"closure":1}'::jsonb, 21, 1, 82, '2026-07-13'::date, 'Anishqa', 'For an EO retreat at Oberoi Rajgarh, Kh